VTrigger: save() echoes the raw flags/filterFlags bytes captured at load
time instead of reconstructing them from the bool members it exposes.

`VTrigger::load` unpacks two deprecated bytes, `flags` and `filter_flags`,
into eight public bools — `start_enabled`, `send_untrigger`,
`react_to_on_trigger`, `react_to_on_touch`, `react_to_on_damage`,
`respond_to_object`, `respond_to_pc`, `respond_to_npc` — and those bools are
what the header documents and what a consumer is expected to set. `save()`,
though, writes `this->flags`/`this->filter_flags` verbatim:

	w.write_raw("flags", reinterpret_cast<std::byte const*>(&this->flags), 1);
	w.write_raw("filterFlags", reinterpret_cast<std::byte const*>(&this->filter_flags), 1);

No setter keeps the two in sync — a `VTrigger` constructed fresh (both bytes
zero-initialized) and given `start_enabled = true` saves `flags == 0`, and a
loaded one that flips a bool and saves gets back whatever the archive held at
load time, silently. This is a writer/reader asymmetry in the same family as
0003's `original_pose` and 0006's copy-paste entry names: the struct exposes
one representation and the writer serializes another, so every consumer of
the public API — not just this binding — loses the write.

Measured against zenkit-node's own `setVobClassProp` round-trip test: setting
`zCTrigger.sendUntrigger` true, saving and reloading reads back `false`,
because the packed byte the writer echoed was never touched.

The fix reconstructs `flags`/`filter_flags` from the bool members immediately
before writing, the exact inverse of `load()`'s unpacking — same bits, same
shifts. It changes nothing about a `VTrigger` that is loaded and saved
untouched, since the reconstructed bytes equal the loaded ones bit-for-bit.

Upstreamable as-is: a plain writer/reader asymmetry, no API change.

diff --git a/src/vobs/Trigger.cc b/src/vobs/Trigger.cc
index 23f9c29..47e4206 100644
--- a/src/vobs/Trigger.cc
+++ b/src/vobs/Trigger.cc
@@ -46,8 +46,25 @@ namespace zenkit {
 	void VTrigger::save(WriteArchive& w, GameVersion version) const {
 		VirtualObject::save(w, version);
 		w.write_string("triggerTarget", this->target);
-		w.write_raw("flags", reinterpret_cast<std::byte const*>(&this->flags), 1);
-		w.write_raw("filterFlags", reinterpret_cast<std::byte const*>(&this->filter_flags), 1);
+		// `flags`/`filter_flags` are the deprecated raw bytes `load()` unpacks into
+		// the bool members below and are not kept in sync with them by any setter —
+		// a caller that sets e.g. `start_enabled` and saves would silently get back
+		// whatever the archive happened to hold at load time (0 for a freshly
+		// constructed object). Reconstruct the packed bytes from the bools here, the
+		// exact inverse of the unpacking in load(), instead of echoing the stale
+		// members.
+		std::uint8_t flags = 0;
+		flags |= this->start_enabled << 0;
+		flags |= this->send_untrigger << 2;
+		std::uint8_t filter_flags = 0;
+		filter_flags |= this->react_to_on_trigger << 0;
+		filter_flags |= this->react_to_on_touch << 1;
+		filter_flags |= this->react_to_on_damage << 2;
+		filter_flags |= this->respond_to_object << 3;
+		filter_flags |= this->respond_to_pc << 4;
+		filter_flags |= this->respond_to_npc << 5;
+		w.write_raw("flags", reinterpret_cast<std::byte const*>(&flags), 1);
+		w.write_raw("filterFlags", reinterpret_cast<std::byte const*>(&filter_flags), 1);
 		w.write_string("respondToVobName", this->vob_target);
 		w.write_int("numCanBeActivated", this->max_activation_count);
 		w.write_float("retriggerWaitSec", this->retrigger_delay_sec);
